## HEAVY (other seats and gates share this machine and ONE lock)
A heavy job = `dotnet restore`, `dotnet build`, `dotnet test`, `dotnet publish`, a run of a test executable, and a loop of one. **Every heavy job goes through `<lane>/gate/heavy.py`, in the foreground, ONE at a time; you never start `dotnet` or a test executable any other way.** A job is `python3 -I '<lane>/gate/heavy.py' '<lane>' 'B1940 seat X39' <label> '<clone>' [K=V ...] -- dotnet <arguments>` (or a test executable's absolute path and `-noColor`, or `/bin/bash '<lane>/scratch/<your loop>.sh' ...`), a new label for every job; `CI=true` and `TIMEOUT=<s>` go before the --. The runner reads load1 FIRST and waits while it is 7 or more (at most 30 minutes, then `BLOCKED: load`), THEN takes the wave's lock by `mkdir` of EXACTLY `/Volumes/Datasec_DEV/!CODING/Datasec/OXPd_2/.tools/wave1-heavy.lock.d` with `owner.txt`, runs the ONE command under a time bound (900 s by default), and releases the lock by MOVING the folder to `<lane>/_quarantine/locks/`. **Seat X36 (lane A2) shares this lock: an `owner.txt` is never yours to break. Allow 30 minutes of waits in all**; `BLOCKED: lock held <its owner line>` after that is a relaunch for Tuesday, not a finding. The drafter ran this runner unchanged for every job (MEASURED: lock waits 0 to 8; a build 2 to 11 s, the whole solution's test about 8 s, the Universal Print executable about 5 s). Never hold the lock while you read, edit or wait on a hosted run. zsh does not split an unquoted variable: write each command out in full or wrap it in `bash -c`.

